{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 240,
    "dDurationMs": 2560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thank you to DeleteMe for Supporting PBS."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3269,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Big things are made of smaller things, \nand those smaller things are made of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 7669,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "smaller things still. That’s reductionism in a \nnutshell, and digging our way to the smallest  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 14549,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "layer has been one of the primary \ngoals of physics for ever. But what if,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 19989,
    "dDurationMs": 8160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "just before we reach the bottom, we \nfind out that reductionism fails?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 31669,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "June is black hole month at the merch \nstore! We have two new black hole merch  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 36709,
    "dDurationMs": 6280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "items for sale. First up is a black hole size \ncomparison desktop and gaming mat. This 32x16  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 42989,
    "dDurationMs": 6680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "inch mat shows a NASA accurate comparison of \nthe supermassive Ton 618 Black Hole compared  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 49669,
    "dDurationMs": 7840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to M87 and Cygnus A. And next is binary \nblack hole orbit t-shirt and sweatshirt,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 57509,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that shows how two supermassive black hole \naccretion disks would warp when viewed from  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 62629,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "near the orbital plane. There’s also 10% \noff everything the merch store in june.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 67989,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Just use the code: blackhole at checkout. \nLinks in the descrption. On to the episode"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 75189,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You are made of cells, and your behavior can be \nthought of as the collective behavior of those  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 79829,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cells—whether it’s the collective throbbing \nof cardiac cells behind your heartbeat or the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 84789,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "coordinated firing of neurons behind your \nsubjective experience. We don’t need cell  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 90469,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "biology to describe your pulse rate—we \njust need a number of beats per minute,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 94709,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and we don’t need the neurophysiology of ion \nchannels and action potentials to understand your  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 100229,
    "dDurationMs": 8000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fear of clowns—psychology and maybe psychiatry are \nmore useful there. If we go deeper, those heart  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 108229,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and brain cells are made of molecules and then \natoms, whose interactions can be understood by  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 113669,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "chemistry without much attention paid to the \nquantum mechanics at work even deeper down."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 120229,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The universe has layers, roughly sorted according \nto size, and each layer can be described by its  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 126069,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "own set of rules or “dynamics” that we can use \nwithout having to know the rules of the layer  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 131909,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "below. It’s pretty convenient that we don’t have \nto do quantum mechanics to understand how people  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 136629,
    "dDurationMs": 7680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "work. And we take this peculiar feature of our \nuniverse for granted, but perhaps we shouldn’t."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 144309,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s also convenient that to understand nature, we \nonly need to look to smaller and smaller scales.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 150709,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Understand the smallest, then in principle \nwe’ve understood the largest. This notion,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 155269,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "broadly described as reductionism, has been the \ncornerstone of much of science and certainly  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 159749,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "physics since the ancient Greeks. But this too \nmay be an assumption that needs to be questioned."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 167029,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In a recent episode, we talked about the \nhierarchy problem—the fact that the Higgs  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 172149,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "particle’s small mass doesn’t feel natural in \nthe context of this reductionist worldview.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 177589,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Some physicists suggest that this is \na strike against reductionism. Others,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 182389,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "like Sabine Hossenfelder who responded to our \nvideo with one of her own, contends that it’s  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 188069,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this notion of “naturalness” that is misused. \nBut reductionism has been a foundational tenet  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 193829,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of physics for ever, and naturalness has \nlong been considered an important tool."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 199749,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This video is going to lay down some \ngroundwork to give you a solid sense  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 204069,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of how these concepts are fundamental \nto much of physics, which will help us  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 209749,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "understand why we should be surprised that these \napproaches seems to fall apart at the bottom."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 215829,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s start by defining some concepts. The \nidea that big things can be understood in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 220709,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "terms of small things is called \nmethodological reductionism.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 225349,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We also have the related concept of theory \nreductionism that I’ll come back to."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 230309,
    "dDurationMs": 8640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A few other terms: a level of new dynamics arising \nfrom a deeper, smaller layer emerges from or is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 238949,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "emergent from that deeper layer. The dynamics—the \nrules describing the layer—arise from the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 245829,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "potentially very different dynamics of its many \nparts. And those parts don’t have the emergent  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 252869,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "dynamics specifically coded in them. If the \nemergent dynamics can be described without  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 258709,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "any reference to the underlying dynamics, we say \nthe emergent system has dynamical independence."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 265349,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Perhaps the simplest example is in \nthermodynamics, where the positions,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 269909,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "momenta of countless particles—literally \n10^27 independent-ish numerical properties  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 276709,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or degrees of freedom in the air in a typical \nroom—reduced to a few tightly related variables:  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 284149,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "temperature, pressure, density and \nvolume for an ideal gas in equilibrium."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 288789,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Or take fluid flow. The Navier-Stokes equations \npredict the time-dependent behavior of fluids  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 294309,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with incredible accuracy, relating the density, \nmomentum, various stresses, etc. of the fluid  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 300709,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with no reference to the nature of the individual \nparticles making up the flow. Their properties  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 305269,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are subsumed in the global parameters of the \nfluid. For example, the maddeningly complex  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 311589,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "attractive interactions between fluid particles \njust becomes a single number: the viscosity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 316869,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There are plenty of other examples where \nconstituent particles of a system have no  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 321269,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "knowledge of the emergent dynamics that come from \nthem, from the patterns within a susurration of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 325829,
    "dDurationMs": 2000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "starlings to quasi-particles formed by electron \nholes in superconductors or by skyrmion knots  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 327829,
    "dDurationMs": 1920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in magnetic dipole arrays."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 329749,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In physics, a theory that works in a particular \nnarrow range of some parameter space is called  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 336229,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "an effective theory. Newtonian gravity is an \neffective theory of the more general general  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 342709,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "relativity, with Newton only working \nin cases of relatively weak gravity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 347829,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The most prominent type of effective theory \nin physics is the effective field theory,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 353029,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which is what it sounds like—an effective \ntheory that’s also a field theory,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 358229,
    "dDurationMs": 2720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "meaning it describes properties \nthat extend through space,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 360949,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "like in fluid dynamics or general \nrelativity or quantum field theories.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 365669,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In an effective field theory, the restriction \non the validity of the theory is size scale."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 372309,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The component parts of an EFT—be they \nparticles of air or quantum elements of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 377589,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the fabric of space—are small compared to the \nemergent field. Zoom in close enough and our  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 384069,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "EFT fails—things like temperature and viscosity \nbecome poorly defined and then meaningless as  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 390549,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we enter the realm of individual elements. But \nzoomed out far enough, the underlying elements  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 396309,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are so numerous and so small compared to the EFT \nscale that this behavior can be averaged over;  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 403189,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "their statistics are highly predictive, \neven if the individual elements are not."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 407829,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We say that an EFT integrates over the many \ndegrees of freedom of its underlying elements.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 414229,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We also call this process coarse-graining, in \nthat our pixel scale is coarse enough that we  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 420149,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "can’t resolve the underlying elements. At \nthis point, we see ensemble parameters,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 425349,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and find relationships between those parameters. \nSometimes those coarse-grained relationships are  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 430549,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "exquisitely reliable and robust, and the erratic \nbehavior of the underlying parts are no longer  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 436869,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "needed to predict the system’s behavior. This \nis the dynamical independence I mentioned."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 442949,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But an effective theory breaks when we move \nbeyond its range of validity. An effective field  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 448789,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "theory breaks down below a certain size, when the \ngraining is too coarse to capture the increasingly  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 455429,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "important behavior of the constituent elements \nof the emergent field. A key requirement for an  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 461349,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "effective field theory to work is a separation \nof scales—it has to be big enough compared to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 466949,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "its parts that it’s not overly sensitive to the \nrandom messiness of those parts Here’s an example  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 472789,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of pushing an effective theory too far: at the \nend of the 19th century, physicists were trying to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 478069,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "calculate the spectrum produced by hot objects—the \nblackbody spectrum—the light radiated by particles  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 485029,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "colliding or vibrating with thermal energy. \nBased on the assumptions of classical physics,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 490789,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "physicists were able to correctly calculate \nthe spectrum produced by hot objects,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 495509,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but only for the low energy, long wavelength end \nof that spectrum—the infrared or IR in the case of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 502629,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Sun’s spectrum. When they tried to calculate \nthe short wavelength ultraviolet, UV side,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 509829,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they found that high energy particles contributed \nfar too much intensity, causing the spectrum to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 515509,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "brighten indefinitely in that direction. The \ncalculations didn’t match reality. This was  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 521669,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the so-called ultraviolet catastrophe. It was \nsolved when Max Planck realised that classical  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 527749,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "physics didn’t give a good description of how \nparticles vibrate, and that description got  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 532629,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "worse the higher the energy. His solution was the \nbirth of quantum mechanics. We’ve talked about it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 539429,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The ultraviolet catastrophe resulted from \npushing classical mechanics beyond its  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 543509,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "limit of applicability. By analogy, we often \ncall the limit of an effective theory the “UV  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 549989,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cutoff”--that’s the energy where it stops working \nand we need to transition to the more general,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 555189,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "more true description. In this case, \nit was quantum mechanics. By the way,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 561429,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "once we’re in the quantum realm, the \nUV cutoff corresponds both to a maximum  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 566389,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "energy of validity and to a minimum \nsize, for reasons we’ve discussed."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 571669,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s step back a second. We can describe the \nworld in terms of effective theories that are  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 577829,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "emergent from deeper theories. Those deeper \ntheories are, in principle, more fundamental,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 582709,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but in general are, themselves, also effective \ntheories with their own UV cutoffs. They are  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 588869,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "course-grainings of something even deeper. This \nprocess of zooming in to smaller and more and more  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 595749,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fundamental layers of reality has led us through \nclassical physics to quantum mechanics with an  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 601269,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ultimate eye to a single, most fundamental \ntheory. Via this methodological reductionism  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 608149,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we hope to also achieve the other type \nof reductionism: theory reductionism,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 612869,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which proposes that all effective theories \nare just different manifestations,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 617029,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or special cases of a single, underlying \nmastering theory—a theory of everything."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 623349,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "At least that was the hope. But we \nsaw hints that this program might  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 628069,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be in trouble when we looked at the hierarchy \nproblem recently. Let me refresh your memory."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 634389,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s go all the way down to the smallest scale \nat which we have a well-tested field theory.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 639749,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That’s the Standard Model of particle physics, \nwhich describes the elementary particles like  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 644389,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "electrons and quarks in terms of oscillations \nin quantum fields. If we accept the premise of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 651429,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "theory reductionism then all of nature should \nbe explainable in the context of a single,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 657269,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "master theory. The Standard Model is not that \ntheory because it doesn’t include gravity.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 662069,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That makes it an effective field theory—a \ncoarse-graining of some deeper theory."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 666629,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But where is the UV cutoff of the Standard \nModel? We could argue that the deepest  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 672629,
    "dDurationMs": 7760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "layer of all could be at the size-scale of the \nPlanck length, at 10^-32m. That’s around 17  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 680389,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "orders of magnitude smaller than the scale of the \nheavier of the Standard Model particles. This is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 686149,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "where we know that general relativity and quantum \nmechanics come into hopeless conflict and so both  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 691029,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of them fail as effective field theories \nand we need a theory of quantum gravity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 696549,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So the quantum gravity scale could be our UV \ncutoff. But many physicists argue that the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 701589,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cutoff is much closer in energy to the Standard \nModel. That makes sense if we take our lead from  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 706469,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the ultraviolet catastrophe. Max Planck solved it \nwhen he showed that contributions to the blackbody  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 712629,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "spectrum by high energy particle oscillations are \nsuppressed in the more “true” quantum theory. This  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 720069,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "suppression kicks in at the same energy that the \nclassical theory stopped making good predictions."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 725909,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So where does the Standard Model stop \nbecoming predictive? Some would argue that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 731029,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it’s when we try to predict particle masses. \nParticle masses are a free parameter of the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 735269,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Standard Model—something measured in labs \nrather than predicted by the theory. But,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 740869,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as good reductionists, we expect that the \nmechanism explaining these masses is hidden  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 745989,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the deeper layer, beneath the Standard \nModel’s UV cutoff. It’s a bit confusing,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 750869,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "because the mechanics of the Standard \nModel—quantum field theory—does actually  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 756309,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "explain mass—at least in principle. Mass arises \nfrom a type of interaction with quantum fields."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 763349,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But, in a manner analogous to the ultraviolet \ncatastrophe, particle mass can become very  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 768709,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "large—even infinite—if you naively add the \ncontributions from high energy oscillations  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 773269,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in those quantum fields. The main culprit is \nthe Higgs boson, which in turn grants mass  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 779269,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to other elementary particles. Rewatch \nthe last video for the nitty gritty."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 784309,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The point here is that the power of the \nStandard Model to explain nature appears  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 789429,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to fail at around the mass of the Higgs boson. \nTaking their queue from, among other things,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 795269,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the ultraviolet catastrophe, many physicists \nexpected to find new physics close to this  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 799669,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mass—physics that would suppress or cancel \nhigh energy contributions. They did not find  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 807109,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that physics. At least, the Large Hadron \nCollider hasn’t yet, and it’s probed  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 811749,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "quite a bit deeper than where this physics was \nsupposed to appear in the form of new particles."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 817349,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So could the mechanics that define the \nStandard Model’s parameters lie much deeper  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 821829,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "than is accessible to the LHC, or perhaps \neven to any plausible future accelerator?  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 827349,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Could this so-called UV theory only emerge many \norders of magnitude larger in energy and smaller  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 833989,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in size than the scale of the Standard \nModel, perhaps even at the Planck scale?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 838629,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Many would say that this feels “unnatural”, \nclaiming it requires very precise tuning  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 843909,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the parameters of that UV theory. In an \nupcoming episode I’ll properly address the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 848389,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fine-tuning argument more rigorously, \nincluding whether we should expect to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 853509,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "expect the Standard Model parameters and \nthe UV parameters from which they emerge  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 857429,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to be anything other than what they are. \nSome, like Sabine, think we should not."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 863749,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For now, I want to end with an illustration of the \nstrangeness of the situation, in terms of size,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 870469,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "not energy. What is the typical separation of \nscales between familiar dynamically independent  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 877349,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "levels? Think starling flocks to individual birds \nto bird cells to molecular machinery to atoms to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 884149,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the elementary particles of the Standard Model. \nWe zoom in 2-4 orders of magnitude each time,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 890069,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and then we have this chasm that could be \nup to 17 orders of magnitude between these  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 895829,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "elementary particles and the deepest layer. \nThere’s as much difference between the Planck  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 902229,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "scale to the scale of the Higgs as there is \nbetween the atom and the blue whale. Imagine  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 908149,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we tried to find out what blue whales are \nmade of and we found no supporting structure  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 913029,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for 17 orders of magnitude—no organs, \nno cells, no molecules. Just atoms of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 918789,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "blue whale that somehow managed to get \ntheir act together to make a cetacean."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 923509,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is a problem because it gives us a sense that \nthe atoms knew what they were trying to build,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 929349,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which contradicts the whole idea of our \nmethodological reductionism. The parts  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 933669,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "should not know about the whole—emergence \ngoes from small to big. This requirement  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 939429,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is a bit more nuanced than that. There IS \nfeedback between the generating and emergent  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 944549,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "layers. For example, a cell can’t live without \nits host organism, molecular machinery requires a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 950949,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "stable environment of the cell, even the lowerly \nquark can only exist in composite particles.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 957029,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There’s a feedback between scales—small \ngenerates large, large stabilizes small."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 963429,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But this feedback acts locally typically each \nscale directly influences a layer above and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 970309,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "stabilizes a layer below—similar \nto how the dynamics within each  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 974469,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "layer is spatially local. Throughout most of \nnature, this interplay between emergence and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 980469,
    "dDurationMs": 7760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "stabilizing feedback works when the separation of \nscales is not too small but also not too large."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 988229,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It's hard to imagine a mechanism for that \nfeedback over the 17 orders of magnitude  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 993589,
    "dDurationMs": 3600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from the Higgs to the quantum \ngravity scale. If this is true,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 997189,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it seems like there are really only \ntwo broad possible explanations. Option  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1002069,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "1 is that the UV theory that ultimately \nunderlies and defines the Standard Model  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1008389,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "just is the way it is. It’s doing its thing, \noblivious to the fact that incredible richness  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1015269,
    "dDurationMs": 7520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "arises many orders of magnitude away in scale. \nThat does seem a bit lucky, or finely tuned.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1022789,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We could also invoke the anthropic principle here, \nin that only universes with such a quality end up  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1029909,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "getting observed - by blue whales presumably. \nThere’s a solid case to be made for this, but  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1036469,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you do need a ridiculous number of universes to \nmake it work. We’ll come back to that some time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1043189,
    "dDurationMs": 9040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Or option 2) Perhaps a type of downward causation \nor large-to-small feedback is stronger than the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1052229,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "standard reductionist paradigm generally \npermits. Perhaps the Higgs does tell the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1058709,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "lower levels how to get their act together \nover vast scales to stabilize its mass,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1063349,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or perhaps the Higgs mass is stabilized \nnot from below but rather from above.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1068789,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The general term for this kind \nof anti-reductionist reversal of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1072949,
    "dDurationMs": 7840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "influence between the small and large, the \nultraviolet and infrared, is UV-IR mixing.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1080789,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And there’s some strong motivation to think \nthat this might be an answer—we already see  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1085829,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "UV-IR mixing in one very familiar case—that \nof gravity. But that’s also for another time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1092869,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In either case, something weird and cool \nis going on at the bottom of reality,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1098549,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and the clue is in the separation \nof reality’s emergent layers,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1102389,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from blue whales down to the \nquantum fabric of spacetime."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1107669,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thank you to DeleteMe for supporting \nPBS. DeleteMe was built to safeguard  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1111829,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "its members from data brokers—companies that \ncompile detailed, interconnected profiles by  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1117109,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "scraping public records, mining social \nmedia, and purchasing commercial data.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1121829,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These profiles are then sold to various \nindustries, usually without the individuals ever  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1126549,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "knowing. So wow does DeleteMe work? Their platform \nsearches through hundreds of data broker sites,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1133989,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "identifies where your information is exposed, and \nissues official removal requests. It also provides  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1141189,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "continuous monitoring to quickly catch and \naddress any resurfacing data as well as protect  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1147109,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and prevent things like doxxing, harassment, \nor identity theft. With a family plan, DeleteMe  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1153509,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "extends these protections to linked individuals—to \nhelp minimize household exposure by disrupting  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1159589,
    "dDurationMs": 8000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "how brokers infer relational connections. You can \nget 20% off any DeleteMe consumer or family plan  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1167589,
    "dDurationMs": 10000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at joindeleteme.com/spacetime with promo code \nspacetime. There’s a link in the description."
    } ]
  } ]
}
